  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Tonight:\n\nA cold front stretches from near Crane Lake southwestward to \nnear Brainerd this afternoon. Ahead of the front, warm \nsouthwesterly flow is breezy at times with a mix of mid and \nhigh level cloudiness. Behind the front, low stratus and \nscattered rain showers are moving southwest to northeast, with \noccasionally breezy north winds. This front will slowly move to \nthe southeast, with winds gradually turning to the northeast \novernight. Winds will be breezy, with the strongest winds near \nthe shoreline. Cannot rule out scattered showers overnight, \nmainly near the border and over the Arrowhead, but for most of \nthe area, cloudy skies are expected. Cooler air arrives behind \nthe front, with lows ranging from the upper 30s near the border \nto around 60F from Siren eastward to Park Falls. \n\nMonday:\n\nTomorrow morning, the front should stretch across northwest WI,\ndraped underneath somewhat zonal flow aloft. There could be \nsome scattered showers around through the morning and early \nafternoon, but more widespread coverage is possible tomorrow \nevening as a stronger shortwave craves out the ridge sitting to \nour south. The shortwave will bring better dynamics and lapse \nrates as temps cool aloft. However, warm advection keeps the \nairmass capped. While shear values are 40-50kts, MUCAPE values \nare meager at around 300 J/kg or less. There is a non- zero \nchance of thunder from Brainerd to Duluth southward by late \nafternoon and evening, with a few non-severe elevated storms \npossible.\n\nTuesday into the end of the work week:\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region for mid week, with more\nseasonal temperatures expected each day. Highs will be in the \n50s and 60s and overnight lows in the 30s. The next system \narrives Thursday, as a southern stream upper low opens up as it \nlifts across the Plains and merges with a northern stream trough\ndropping through central Canada. Deterministic models have come\ninto better agreement today with each other and ensemble \nguidance, showing a double surface low set up, with moisture \nstreaming northward into the Midwest. There remains timing \ndifferences, with the moisture advection spreading more toward \nthe southern Great Lakes. This would limit precipitation chances\nfor the Northland. Drier conditions return for the weekend.",
